# Catalog & assets

| Role | Path |
|------|------|
| Incremental drop zone | `new_input/` — batch xlsx + product png only |
| Drop-zone sheets | `catalog` (new rows) + `categories` (existing category1/category2 reference) |
| Full master workbook | `data/commodity_idx.xlsx` |
| Product images | `public/products/` |
| UI logo / icons | `public/brand/`, `public/icons/` (never via new_input) |
| Runtime JSON | `src/shared/data/catalog.json` (generated) |

## Excel rules

- `id` = unique SKU
- One `name` ↔ one `picture` file
- Columns: `id | category1 | category2 | name | size_mm | high_mm | price_twd | picture | supply`
- `size_mm`: occupancy along the bracelet cord (beads = diameter; **pendants = bail/hook width only**, typically ~2mm). On-cord accessories also use this as max drawn length along the ring.
- `high_mm`: beads = same as `size_mm`; **pendants = bail-top → tip height** (uniform scale on canvas, aspect preserved); other accessories = max height perpendicular to the cord (canvas may stretch).
- `supply`: supplier name (optional)
- Same picture + different `size_mm` / `high_mm` / price = different SKUs
- Bead / mixed batches: `new_input` workbook = **delta only**; sync upserts into `data/`
- **Accessory-only** batches: xlsx is the full accessory source of truth — master accessories not listed are deleted

## Sync

```bash
npm run sync:catalog
```

Sync copies batch images into `public/products/`, then **normalizes** each PNG so bead content fills ~90% of a square (avoids uneven source padding). Re-run all images with `npm run normalize:products`.

Do not hand-edit `catalog.json`. Do not store logo/svg in `new_input/`.
Do not add a README under `new_input/`. After sync, xlsx stays with headers only.
